---
import InlineCommand from "../../components/InlineCommand.tsx";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Adding a Skill - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="practical-exercise" subChapterId="adding-a-skill">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        In this chapter, you'll install the <code>skill-creator</code> skill and use it to build a custom "Requirements Writing" skill. This skill will be based
        on the existing project documentation, ensuring the AI consistently follows the project's standards.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-violet-50 border border-violet-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
        <div class="text-xs font-bold text-violet-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Recall: Why use a skill?</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-violet-800 leading-relaxed">
          Creating a skill instead of just using a prompt allows for <strong>progressive disclosure</strong> as well as reusability of the prompt/skill. The AI only uses the full set of
          requirement-writing instructions when it identifies that you are starting a requirements task. This keeps your context window clean and prevents the
          AI from being overloaded with irrelevant instructions during other tasks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 1 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Install the Skill Creator</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          First, we need to add the <code>skill-creator</code> skill to your project. This tool is designed specifically to help you build skills for AI agents like
          Gemini CLI.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-2 font-medium">Run the following command in your terminal:</p>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">
          {`npx skills add https://github.com/anthropics/skills --skill skill-creator`}
        </div>

        <div class="bg-teal-50 border border-teal-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-teal-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Verify Installation</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-teal-800 leading-relaxed mb-3">
            To confirm the <code>skill-creator</code> has been added correctly:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2 mb-0">
            <li class="text-sm text-teal-800">
              <strong>Check the skills list:</strong> Run <InlineCommand command="/skills" client:visible /> in your Gemini CLI and ensure <strong
                >skill-creator</strong
              > is present
            </li>
            <li class="text-sm text-teal-800">
              <strong>Verify the directory:</strong> Check that a <code>skill-creator</code> directory exists in your <code>.agents/skills/</code> folder.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 2 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Installation Process</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">The CLI will prompt you for several configuration options.</p>

        <ul class="text-sm text-stone-700 space-y-3 mb-6">
          <li class="text-sm text-stone-700">
            <strong>Agent Selection:</strong> Select your current agent from the list. If your agent is already covered by the <code>Universal</code> selection, you
            can proceed to the next step.
          </li>
          <li class="text-sm text-stone-700">
            <strong>Installation Scope:</strong> Choose <strong>Project</strong>. This ensures the skill is available specifically for this sample project.
          </li>
          <li class="text-sm text-stone-700">
            <strong>Installation Method:</strong> Select the recommended method (likely <strong>Symlink</strong>). This allows the tool to update the skill
            files in place.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="border border-red-200 bg-red-50 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-red-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Skip Skill Finder</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-red-800 leading-relaxed">
            During the installation process, you may be prompted to install a "skill finder" skill. Do not install it at this time. Focus only on the <code
              >skill-creator</code
            >.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 3 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">3</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Create a Requirements Writing Skill</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          Now you'll use the <code>skill-creator</code> to build a specialized skill for writing requirements.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-4">
          Ask your AI agent to create a new skill named <strong>"Requirements Writing"</strong>.
        </p>

        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          When creating the skill, instruct the AI to use the existing documentation found in <InlineCommand
            command="@docs/requirements"
            client:visible
          />. This means the skill's instructions should reflect the structure, tone, and level of detail found in the requirement files in that directory. (feel free to request it do these things)
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          Now is also the time to add more details of how you want it to write requirements, where they should go and if you want to add in any changes to existing requirements. Build something that will work for you as part of this exercise.
        </p>

        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          At the start of a session the Gemini CLI reads only the names and descriptions of skills from the <code>SKILL.md</code> file. The AI then looks for a match
          and will automatically load the skill if your prompt matches the conditions in the description (or you can explicitly mention the skill name if it seems
          like the AI is missing the hint).
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600">
          Once created, you should see a new directory for your skill (e.g., in <code>.agents/skills/requirements-writing/</code>) containing a <code
            >SKILL.md</code
          > file.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-6">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">What to expect</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-amber-800 leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Because we used the <code>skill-creator</code> skill to create the <code>requirements-skill</code>, there are a few things to bear in mind:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2 mb-0">
            <li class="text-sm text-amber-800">
              The AI will likely stop at various points throughout its execution and ask for your confirmation and maybe ask questions along the way.
            </li>
            <li class="text-sm text-amber-800">
             Since we used the <code>skill-creator</code> skill, to ensure the newly generated <code
                >requirements-skill</code
              > is accurate, the AI will execute a self-validation loop. It generates mock requirements and grades its own output against the established project
              standards found in your documentation.
            </li>
            <li class="text-sm text-amber-800">
              <i>Had we simply prompted the AI to write a requirements skill manually, these validation steps would be missing but you can still get a good skill written by pointing to documentation of how a skill is written or pointing it at an existing skill.</i>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-teal-50 border border-teal-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-10">
        <div class="text-xs font-bold text-teal-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Check for Success</div>
        <ul class="text-sm text-teal-800 space-y-1 mt-2">
          <li class="text-sm text-teal-800">
            ✓ <code>skill-creator</code> and <code>requirements-writing</code> directories can be found under <code>.agents/skills</code>.
          </li>
          <li class="text-sm text-teal-800">
            ✓ The requirements writing <code>SKILL.md</code> file explicitly references the requirement standards from the sample project.
          </li>
          <li class="text-sm text-teal-800">
            ✓ Read the skill and see how it's written. This is a good time to make tweaks if you want to.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="prose space-y-4">
        <h3>Before Continuing</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600">
          You've now installed an external skill and used it to create a custom skill for the project's workflow. However, it's important to remember that while
          AI is excellent at using the tools and skills it's provided, its effectiveness is directly tied to the quality of the <strong>golden masters</strong> you
          provide (in this case, the project requirements). Without providing a strong starting point, the AI's output will require significant manual tweaking (often
          taking more time than if you had written the requirement yourself from scratch).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
